1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:05,958
Chapter 58. Brit.

2
00:00:05,959 --> 00:00:10,435
Steering northeastward from the Croettes, we fell in with vast meadows

3
00:00:10,436 --> 00:00:15,799
of Brit, the minute yellow substance upon which the right whale largely

4
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:21,318
feeds, for leagues and leagues had undulated round us, so that we seemed to

5
00:00:21,319 --> 00:00:26,999
be sailing through boundless fields of ripe and golden wheat. On the second

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,115
day, numbers of right whales were seen, who, secure from the attack of a sperm

7
00:00:32,116 --> 00:00:37,638
whaler like the Peekquad, with open jaws sluggishly swam through the Brit, which

8
00:00:37,639 --> 00:00:42,838
adhering to the fringing fibers of that wondrous Venetian blind in their mouths,

9
00:00:42,839 --> 00:00:47,799
was in that manner separated from the water that escaped at the lip.

10
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:52,679
as morning mowers who side by side slowly and seethingly advanced their

11
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:59,238
sides through the long wet grass of marshy mess. Even so these monsters swam

12
00:00:59,239 --> 00:01:04,755
making a strange grassy cutting sound and leaving behind them endless swaths

13
00:01:04,756 --> 00:01:08,198
of blue upon the yellow sea.

14
00:01:08,199 --> 00:01:13,878
Footnote. That part of the sea known among whalemen as the Brazil banks does

15
00:01:13,879 --> 00:01:18,359
not bear that name as the banks of Newfoundland do because of there being

16
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:22,838
shallows and soundings there, but because of this remarkable meadow-like

17
00:01:22,839 --> 00:01:27,878
appearance caused by the vast drifts of Brit continually floating in those

18
00:01:27,879 --> 00:01:34,039
latitudes where the right whale is often chased. End of footnote.

19
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:38,435
But it was only the sound they made as they parted the Brit, which at all

20
00:01:38,436 --> 00:01:43,558
reminded one of mowers, seen from the mast heads, especially when they paused

21
00:01:43,559 --> 00:01:48,594
and were stationary for a while, their vast black forms looked more like

22
00:01:48,595 --> 00:01:53,799
lifeless masses of rock than anything else. And as in the great hunting

23
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:58,435
countries of India, the stranger at a distance will sometimes pass on the

24
00:01:58,436 --> 00:02:03,958
plains recumbent elephants without knowing them to be such, taking them for

25
00:02:03,959 --> 00:02:09,958
bare blackened elevations of the soil, even so often for him who for the first

26
00:02:09,959 --> 00:02:16,039
time beholds this species of Leviathans on the sea. And even when recognized at

27
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:21,399
last, their immense magnitude renders it very hard really to believe that such

28
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:26,914
bulky masses of overgrowth can possibly be instinct in all parts with the same

29
00:02:26,915 --> 00:02:31,079
sort of life that lives in a dog or a horse.

30
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:35,958
Indeed, in other respects, you can hardly regard any creatures of the deep

31
00:02:35,959 --> 00:02:40,914
with the same feelings that you do of those of the shore. For though some old

32
00:02:40,915 --> 00:02:45,399
naturalists have maintained that all creatures of the land are of their kind

33
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:50,838
in the sea, and though taking a broad general view of the thing, this may very

34
00:02:50,839 --> 00:02:56,115
well be, yet coming to specialties, where, for example, does the ocean

35
00:02:56,116 --> 00:03:01,399
furnish any fish that in disposition answers to the sagacious kindness of the

36
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:07,399
dog? The oursed shark alone can in any generic respect be said to bear

37
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:12,838
comparative analogy to him. But though to landsmen in general the native

38
00:03:12,839 --> 00:03:17,638
inhabitants of the seas have ever been regarded with emotions unspeakably

39
00:03:17,639 --> 00:03:22,914
unsocial and repelling, though we know the sea to be an everlasting terror

40
00:03:22,915 --> 00:03:28,359
incognita, so that Columbus sailed over numberless unknown worlds to discover

41
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:34,518
his one superficial western one. Though by vast odds, the most horrific of all

42
00:03:34,519 --> 00:03:39,719
mortal disasters have immemorialally and indiscriminately befallen tens and

43
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:44,594
hundreds of thousands of those who have gone upon the waters. Though but a

44
00:03:44,595 --> 00:03:50,274
moment's consideration will teach that however baby man may brag of his science

45
00:03:50,275 --> 00:03:55,719
and skill and however much in a flattering future that science and skill

46
00:03:55,720 --> 00:04:01,799
may augment, yet forever and forever to the crack of doom the sea will insult

47
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:07,719
and murder him and pulverize the statiest stiffest frigot he can make.

48
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:12,359
Nevertheless, by the continual repetition of these very impressions,

49
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:20,198
man has lost that sense of the full awfulness of the sea which Aboriginally belongs to it.

50
00:04:20,199 --> 00:04:25,878
The first boat we read of floated on an ocean that with Portuguese vengeance had

51
00:04:25,879 --> 00:04:31,159
wellemed a whole world without leaving so much as a widow. That same ocean

52
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:37,638
rolls now. That same ocean destroyed the wrecked ships of last year. Yay! Foolish

53
00:04:37,639 --> 00:04:45,558
mortals, Noah's flood is not yet subsided. Twothirds of the fair world it yet covers.

54
00:04:45,559 --> 00:04:50,838
Wherein differ the sea and the land that a miracle upon one is not a miracle upon

55
00:04:50,839 --> 00:04:56,435
the other. Predternatural terrors rested upon the Hebrews, when under the feet of

56
00:04:56,436 --> 00:05:01,638
Kora and his company the live ground opened and swallowed them up forever.

57
00:05:01,639 --> 00:05:07,079
Yet not a modern sun ever sets, but in precisely the same manner, the live sea

58
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:10,518
swallows up ships and crews.

59
00:05:10,519 --> 00:05:13,079
But not only is the sea such a foe to

60
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:18,594
man who is an alien to it, but it is also a fiend to its own offspring, worse

61
00:05:18,595 --> 00:05:23,238
than the Persian host who murdered his own guests, sparing not the creatures

62
00:05:23,239 --> 00:05:28,838
which itself hath spawned. Like a savage tigress that tossing in the jungle

63
00:05:28,839 --> 00:05:34,198
overlays her own cubs, so the sea dashes even the mightiest whales against the

64
00:05:34,199 --> 00:05:40,518
rocks and leaves them there side by side with the split wrecks of ships. No

65
00:05:40,519 --> 00:05:46,518
mercy, no power, but its own controls it. Panting and snorting like a mad

66
00:05:46,519 --> 00:05:53,638
battle steed that has lost its rider, the masterless ocean overruns the globe.

67
00:05:53,639 --> 00:05:58,518
Consider the subtleness of the sea, how its most dreaded creatures glide

68
00:05:58,519 --> 00:06:03,318
underwater, unapparent for the most part, and treacherously hidden beneath

69
00:06:03,319 --> 00:06:09,079
the loveliest tints of azure. Consider also the devilish brilliance and beauty

70
00:06:09,080 --> 00:06:14,518
of many of its most remorseless tribes as the dainty embellished shape of many

71
00:06:14,519 --> 00:06:20,914
species of sharks. Consider once more the universal cannibalism of the sea,

72
00:06:20,915 --> 00:06:28,274
all whose creatures prey upon each other, carrying on eternal war since the world began.

73
00:06:28,275 --> 00:06:34,435
Consider all this, and then turn to this green, gentle, and most dosile earth.

74
00:06:34,436 --> 00:06:39,318
Consider them both, the sea and the land, and do you not find a strange

75
00:06:39,319 --> 00:06:44,679
analogy to something in yourself? For as this appalling ocean surrounds the

76
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:50,999
verdant land, so in the soul of man, there lies one insular Tahiti full of

77
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:57,399
peace and joy, but encompassed by all the horrors of the half-known life. God

78
00:06:57,400 --> 00:07:00,838
keep thee. Push not off from that aisle.

79
00:07:00,839 --> 00:07:05,040
Thou canst never return.
